WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Do you remember any hurricanes or floods in the Ninth Ward?

2
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:11.280
Yes, I remember one in the '40s and we didn't . . . We didn't leave the house.

3
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.660
We were on Pauline Street.

4
00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.920
We didn't leave the house.

5
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:20.200
We stayed there and it was just like a real bad heavy rain.

6
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.800
It wasn't like, you know, Katrina where this flooded and that flooded.

7
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:33.340
My father lived through the 1900s storm in Galveston and he would tell us something about it.

8
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:38.600
And we were kids, and I'm so sorry I told him, I'd say, "Oh I don't want to hear about that."

9
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:46.200
But he would tell us some of his experiences there and frankly that gave me a great deal of strength.

10
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.000
We stayed in New Orleans nine days after Katrina.

11
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.020
And his memory and thinking of it gave me a great deal of strength.

12
00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.660
Were you living here during Katrina?

13
00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.900
In this house.

14
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.680
Nine days.

15
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.340
There were about nine people that lived . . . that stayed.

16
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:13.120
I would say six white and three black.

17
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.820
However, for the nine days, you weren't a man, you weren't a woman, you weren't old,

18
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:25.240
and you weren't young, and for sure you weren't black, and you weren't white.

19
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.660
We all helped each other.

20
00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.060
We held each other when we cried.

21
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.620
We shared food.

22
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.540
We shared water.

23
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:40.040
And it was a very, very close community.

24
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:54.080
About seven of them left and my husband said everything was going to go back to normal in three days and then a policeman came and he had tears in his eyes and he said, "Please leave."

25
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.140
We said, "Okay."

26
00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.000
So that's when we went up to Abita Springs.

27
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.260
And when did you return to the Ninth Ward?

28
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.340
About a month later when they had potable water.

29
00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:04.700
Right.

30
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:05.480
Wow.

31
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.880
But I'll never stay again.

32
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:14.640
I kept a journal of it and I haven't even . . . I don't want to see it, I haven't looked at it.

33
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.200
It's something I went through and I'm glad I went through it.

34
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.920
I'm proud that I went through it, but I don't want to do it again.
